ing\n\n## 0. Status statement (design-only)\nThis is a complete, executable-by-specification protocol. **No experiment was run and no empirical outcome is claimed or reported** (per task context: \"Do not claim that an experiment was run\"). All figures/effects below are *targets and decision criteria*, not results. Every consequential choice is explicitly labeled **[ASSUMPTION]**; everything required by the brief is a fixed requirement.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Model/task choice [ASSUMPTION, stated]\n**Choice:** a small **decoder-only GPT-2-style transformer**: 6 layers, 8 attention heads, d_model=192, d_ff=768, learned word embeddings, un-tied LM head, \u22486.5M parameters; finetuned with next-token cross-entropy on a \u224810M-token corpus (a subset of TinyStories, else a local/deterministic substitute, since the workspace ships no datasets and network is disabled).\n\n**One-sentence rationale:** BAdam (block-coordinate-descent over Adam, Luo et al. 2024) is designed for memory-efficient **full LLM finetuning**, so a small decoder-only LM finetuned on free text is the most representative target; and at this scale both full-parameter training and per-step SVD are trivially cheap on the supplied 224 CPUs (no GPU needed).\n\n**Representativeness justification:** the architecture contains exactly the module families the question asks about \u2014 multi-head attention projections (Q/K/V/O) and MLP projections (up/down + gate) \u2014 organized per layer over an entire training trajectory, which is the setting in which \"learning perturbations\" during finetuning are studied. A single small model is a deliberate scope decision: the design's statistical power comes from *many matched (pass\u2011index) samples per module* rather than from model breadth. **[ASSUMPTION]** \u2014 a bigger model / multiple seeds would raise cost without changing the protocol's logic.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Inspected modules and inclusion/exclusion rule\n**Inspected (2D weight matrices only), per layer.** In each of the 6 layers, the following 7 matrices:\n`W_Q, W_K, W_V` (each [d_model \u00d7 d_model] = [192\u00d7192]; heads concatenated), `W_O` [192\u00d7192], `W_up` [192\u00d7768], `W_gate` [192\u00d7768], `W_down` [768\u00d7192].\n\u2192 Total **42 matrices \u00d7 2 optimizers**, grouped into **7 module types**: {Q, K, V, O, up, gate, down}.\n\n**Excluded, with rule:**\n- **All 1-D parameters** \u2014 biases and LayerNorm \u03b3/\u03b2 (no singular-value spectrum exists). *Explicit rule:* spectral analysis applies only to tensors with both dimensions \u2265 2.\n- **Input/token embeddings and the un-tied LM-head weight.** Rule: these are very large, dominated by sparse/frequency-structured signal, and would dominate any aggregation of effective rank; they are **excluded from the primary analysis** and, if at all, reported in an appendix table, never pooled with the 42 projection matrices. **[ASSUMPTION]**\n- **Adam internal moments (m, v, state)** are *not* perturbati ons \u2014 only their combined effect on W is measured.\n\n**Learning-perturbation naming:** the \"learning perturbation\" of module m over a horizon is the **total applied weight change \u0394W_m over that horizon** (see \u00a73). This matches the question's \"perturbation ... during finetuning\" \u2014 it is a training-trajectory object sampled at every pass, not a final-checkpoint-only object.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Perturbation extraction (exact formulas, aligned horizon)\n\n### 3.1 Chosen BAdam variant and block partition [ASSUMPTION]\n- **Variant:** block-coordinate-descent **AdamW** (as in Luo et al. 2024): parameters are partitioned into D contiguous blocks; at each \"block step\" only the active block's parameters are updated by an inner AdamW step, all others frozen; blocks follow a **cyclic schedule** b(s) = s mod D, with a block-coordinator **warm-up** equal to one full cycle. Per-parameter Adam (m,v) state is retained across block steps (updated only when its block is active).\n- **Block partition:** **D = 6 blocks, one entire transformer layer per block** (all attention + MLP + LN + biases of that layer). Embeddings/LM-head are **frozen** during training (they are excluded from analysis anyway; freezing is chosen for realism of memory savings and clean layer-blocks). **[ASSUMPTION]** \u2014 D \u2208 {3, 12} is a sensitivity condition (\u00a76).\n- **Inner steps M:** default **M = 1** AdamW inner step per active block per block step, so that one *aligned cycle* gives every module exactly **one** optimizer update. M \u2208 {1,4} sensitivity (\u00a76).\n\n### 3.2 The effective (Adam) update \u2014 shared building block\nAt any Adam(-W) update step s acting on parameter vector \u03b8, the applied change is the **effective step**\n`\u0394\u03b8(s) = \u2212\u03b7_s \u00b7 m\u0302_s / (\u221av\u0302_s + \u03b5)   (+ decoupled weight-decay term \u2212\u03b7_s\u00b7\u03bb\u00b7\u03b8_s, if AdamW)`\nwith bias-corrected first/second moments m\u0302, v\u0302, learning rate \u03b7_s, \u03b5=1e-8, \u03bb (see \u00a75). Reshaped to a matrix W_m this is the per-step **raw perturbation** `\u0394W_m(s)`.\n\n### 3.3 Aligned horizon / block-cycle definition (PRIMARY comparison)\nDefine a **parameter pass** (the natural matching unit) = a complete sweep over all blocks:\n- **Adam:** 1 optimizer step = 1 pass (all modules updated once).\n- **BAdam (M=1):** D = 6 block steps = 1 cycle = 1 pass (each module updated exactly once).\n\nFor cycle/pass index \u03c4, compute the **aligned perturbation**:\n- Adam: `\u0394W_m^{(pass)}(\u03c4) = \u0394W_m(t_\u03c4)` (single step).\n- BAdam: `\u0394W_m^{(cycle)}(\u03c4) = \u03a3_{s \u2208 cycle \u03c4} \u0394W_m(s) = \u0394W_m(s_\u03c4^m)` (the single nonzero block-step update; sum collapses to one term). \n\nBoth are **the total weight change of module m over one full parameter pass**, i.e., the matched, fair object. **This per-cycle/per-pass aligned comparison is the PRIMARY analysis**, because the question asks about the perturbation's spectrum across transformer modules and per-cycle is the only setting in which both optimizers give every module the same nominal amount of update. Any full-model perturbation over an aligned pass has the same number of nonzero module contributions in both optimizers.\n\n### 3.4 Per-step variant (SENSITIVITY, caveated) [ASSUMPTION]\nSecondary condition: compare **single-step raw updates** \u2014 Adam `\u0394W_m(t)` vs. BAdam `\u0394W_m(s)` for the block-step in which m is active (and, for completeness, vs. 0 for inactive blocks at that step). **Explicit caveat (must appear in the report):** per-step BAdam updates are *structurally block-sparse* \u2014 a full-model perturbation at one block step has 5 of 6 layer-blocks exactly zero \u2014 so per-step BAdam effective rank is **low by construction**, not by learning dynamics. This condition is a **mechanism sanity check**, explicitly labeled as NOT interpretable as evidence about comparability of the learning perturbation per se.\n\n### 3.5 Spectral decomposition\nFor each (optimizer, module m, pass \u03c4): `\u0394W_m = U \u03a3 V\u1d40`, order singular values `\u03c3\u2081 \u2265 \u03c3\u2082 \u2265 \u2026 \u2265 \u03c3_r \u2265 0`, `r = min(dim\u2080,dim\u2081)`.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Metrics and pre-registered decision rule\n\n### 4.1 Heavy-tailed singular-value distribution \u2014 three estimators (cross-checked)\nLet {\u03c3\u1d62} be the singular values of the (normalized, \u00a75) perturbation matrix.\n1. **Hill estimator** on the top-k order statistics:\n   `\u03b1\u0302_Hill = [ (1/k) \u03a3_{i=1}^{k} log(\u03c3_i / \u03c3_{k+1}) ]\u207b\u00b9`, with **default k = \u230a0.1\u00b7r\u230b**, grid k \u2208 {\u230a0.05r\u230b, \u230a0.1r\u230b, \u230a0.2r\u230b} for sensitivity. **[ASSUMPTION]** (standard Hill; k chosen to balance tail purity vs. variance).\n2. **Log\u2013log spectral slope:** OLS fit of `log \u03c3\u1d62 = a + b\u00b7log i` on i=1..k; report `\u03b2 = \u2212b`. Cross-check of tail steepness vs. light-tailed baseline. **[ASSUMPTION]** (fit range = same top-k).\n3. **Power-law (Clauset\u2013Shalizi\u2013Newman) MLE** over the tail above a threshold \u03c3_min chosen by KS-minimization:\n   `\u03b1\u0302_CSN = 1 + n\u00b7[ \u03a3_{i: \u03c3\u1d62\u2265\u03c3_min} log(\u03c3\u1d62/\u03c3_min) ]\u207b\u00b9`.\n   **[ASSUMPTION]** (added to guard against Hill's sensitivity; agreement of \u22652 of 3 estimators required to declare an \u03b1).\n- **Heavy-tailed flag (calibration rule):** declared if \u03b1\u0302 \u2208 (2,4) and \u03b2 is significantly shallower than the Gaussian-null slope for the same shape (bootstrap of the null, \u00a74.3). Rationale: this is the empirically observed \u03b1-window for heavy-tailed SGD-type update spectra; a Gaussian matrix gives an effectively infinite/undefined tail index. **[ASSUMPTION]**\n\n### 4.2 Effective rank \u2014 two measures (primary = entropy rank)\nLet `p\u1d62 = \u03c3\u1d62\u00b2 / \u03a3\u2c7c \u03c3\u2c7c\u00b2` (normalized squared singular values, i.e., spectral weight of the operator).\n- **Entropy rank** (primary): `ER = exp( \u2212 \u03a3\u1d62 p\u1d62 \u00b7 log p\u1d62 )` (Roy\u2013Vetterli effective rank of the update operator).\n- **Participation ratio** (secondary cross-check): `PR = (\u03a3\u1d62 \u03c3\u1d62)\u00b2 / (\u03a3\u1d62 \u03c3\u1d62\u00b2)`.\nBoth are **scale-invariant** (invariant to constant rescaling of \u0394W), so \u00a75 normalization does not affect them.\n\n### 4.3 Unambiguous pre-registered comparability rule (per module type) [ASSUMPTION]\nApplied **independently for each of the 7 module types** (Q,K,V,O,up,gate,down), using the **aligned (pass/cycle) samples**:\n- **(R1) Tail-index comparability:** the matched-pairs bootstrap 95% CI (resample *paired* pass-indices \u03c4, B=2000) of the difference `\u0394\u03b1 = \u03b1\u0302_BAdam(\u03c4) \u2212 \u03b1\u0302_Adam(\u03c4)` **contains 0**. (Equivalently: overlap of the two separate bootstrap CIs. The matched-difference CI is primary.)\n- **(R2) Effective-rank comparability:** the matched-pairs bootstrap 95% CI of `log(ER_BAdam/ER_Adam)` is **contained within [\u2212log C, +log C]** with **pre-registered C = 1.5**. **[ASSUMPTION]** (i.e., BAdam effective rank within a factor C=1.5 of Adam, on the log scale).\n- **Verdict per module type:** **\"comparable\"** iff R1 AND R2 hold at the aligned comparison; **\"not comparable\"** iff either fails; **\"inconclusive\"** iff bootstrap CIs are too wide (span > 2\u00d7 the effect of interest, or R2's CI fully straddles but is enormous).\n- **No single global Yes/No** \u2014 the question says \"across various transformer modules,\" so the **deliverable is a per-module-type verdict table** (7 rows), plus a summary row (e.g., fraction of module types \"comparable\"). Multiplicity: report whether verdicts change under Holm\u2013Bonferroni-adjusted \u03b1 for the 7 per-type tests; interpretation is primarily per-type. **[ASSUMPTION]**\n\n---\n\n## 5. Controls and confound controls (full matched set) + normalization rule\n\n**Matched across optimizers (identical for BAdam and Adam):**\n- One shared random **initialization** (same seed); same data ordering/batches (same shuffle + same batch index stream); same batch (16 \u00d7 512 tokens), sequence length, gradient clipping (1.0), dropout (0.1).\n- Optimizer hyperparameters: \u03b7=1e-4 with linear warm-up + cosine decay over the whole run; \u03b2\u2081=0.9, \u03b2\u2082=0.999, \u03b5=1e-8, decoupled weight decay \u03bb=0.01 (AdamW semantics in both). Total **P = 100 parameter passes** (Adam: 100 steps; BAdam: 600 block steps), samples taken at **every pass** (trajectory-wide, not just final). **[ASSUMPTION]**\n- Same gradient-computation code path / same forward-backward; only the optimizer's step application differs (freeze/unfreeze per block for BAdam).\n- BAdam-specific matched defaults: D=6, M=1, cyclic order, 1-cycle coordinator warm-up (\u00a73.1).\n\n**Normalization & noise floor applied before spectral fitting (mandatory rule):**\n- **Scale control:** before SVD, rescale each update matrix by its Frobenius norm: `\u0394W\u0303_m = \u0394W_m / \u2016\u0394W_m\u2016_F`. This removes overall update-magnitude differences (a real confound: a BAdam cycle and an Adam pass can have different total magnitudes) so that the *spectral shape* is compared, not magnitude. **[ASSUMPTION]** \u2014 sensitivity condition runs the no-normalization and spectral-norm variants (\u00a76). Rank metrics (ER, PR) are unaffected by this rescale (scale-invariant), which the report will note explicitly.\n- **Noise floor:** drop singular values below `\u03c3_noise = \u03b5_mach \u00b7 \u2016\u0394W_m\u2016_F \u00b7 max(dim\u2080,dim\u2081)` before tail fitting and rank computation (prevents numerical-noise singular values from creating spurious heavy tails / inflating rank); report the number dropped per matrix. **[ASSUMPTION]**\n- **Magnitude is not a test quantity:** raw per-pass \u2016\u0394W_m\u2016_F for both optimizers is reported as a descriptive covariate table, but is **not** part of the comparability verdict.\n\n**Null/calibration baselines (controls, not test targets):**\n1. **Gaussian null:** i.i.d. N(0,1) matrix of the module shape \u2192 light tail / flat spectrum; calibrates \u03b1\u0302 and the \"heavy-tailed\" flag (expected: no (2,4) \u03b1).\n2. **Isotropic null:** equal singular values (ER = r) \u2192 calibrates rank ceiling.\n3. **Seed-level calibration (primary control):** **Adam run twice under two independent data/init seeds**; the BAdam-vs-Adam verdicts must be interpreted against this **Adam-vs-Adam natural-variability band** (same R1/R2 statistics applied to Adam-A vs Adam-B). If BAdam-vs-Adam differences are within the Adam-vs-Adam band, that is direct evidence that BAdam is as \"comparable\" as Adam is to itself. **[ASSUMPTION]** (added cost ~1 extra Adam run).\n\n---\n\n## 6. Analysis / statistical plan and sensitivity analyses\n\n**Data collected per (optimizer, module m, pass \u03c4):** \u03c3-vector (post-noise-floor), \u03b1\u0302_Hill, \u03b1\u0302_CSN, \u03b2, ER, PR, \u2016\u0394W\u2016_F.\n\n**Primary workflow:**\n1. Per (optimizer, module type g, pass): aggregate (median) across the 6 matrices in type g? **No** \u2014 keep matrix-level samples; group by type g at the statistical stage. So samples = (module matrix, pass) pairs = 6\u00d7100=600 per optimizer per type.\n2. Per type g: **median over passes** with **bootstrap 95% CIs** (B=2000, resample passes) for each of \u03b1, ER, PR, \u03b2 (both optimizers, aligned pass indices).\n3. **Matched difference statistics** (paired by pass \u03c4 within the same module matrix, using the 3 shared seeds' Adam as a band): bootstrap CI of \u0394\u03b1 and of log-ER-ratio; secondary **Wilcoxon signed-rank** on matched differences (report the test statistic, not just p).\n4. **Verdict** per type g by pre-registered rule (\u00a74.3), with the Adam-vs-Adam band (\u00a75.3) displayed alongside.\n5. Report as **per-module-type comparability table** + trajectory plots of \u03b1\u0302(\u03c4) and ER(\u03c4) over passes.\n\n**Sensitivity analyses (each a separate column in the results table, clearly labeled):**\n- (S1) **Per-step vs. per-cycle BAdam** (caveat documented: construction-induced low rank in per-step; primary interpretation restricted to per-cycle).\n- (S2) BAdam inner steps **M \u2208 {1,4}**.\n- (S3) Block partition **D \u2208 {6, 3, 12}** (coarse/fine blocks affect per-cycle definition).\n- (S4) Hill **k \u2208 {0.05r, 0.1r, 0.2r}**; require \u22652 of 3 estimators (Hill, CSN, slope) to agree before calling an \u03b1.\n- (S5) Normalization variant: none vs. Frobenius vs. spectral-norm rescaling.\n- (S6) Horizon stacking: perturbations accumulated over **1 vs. 3 aligned passes** (does accumulation change tail/rank signatures?).\n- (S7) Seed robustness: the 3-seed Adam band + BAdam at a 2nd seed (small extra cost).\n\n**Robustness of conclusions:** a verdict \"comparable\" for a module type is considered robust iff it survives S2\u2013S6 without changing state (comparable \u2194 not); otherwise reported as \"sensitive.\"\n\n---\n\n## 7. Feasibility statement (consistent with design-only)\n- **No execution, no fabricated results:** this deliverable is the protocol; nothing herein is an empirical outcome. The environment (Python 3.14.4, no torch/numpy/scipy, no network, no GPU, `datasets: []`) cannot run this as-is and is not required to, per `track.md`: *\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"*\n- **If executed (informational cost model):** 6.5M-param model; P=100 passes \u2192 100 Adam steps + 600 BAdam block steps + 2 Adam replication runs; each step <1 s on the 224 CPUs; SVDs of \u2264192\u00d7768 matrices are negligible; wall-clock estimate a few hours single-core / well under an hour on available parallelism. Dataset/tokenizer would need to be supplied or synthesized locally (no network); dependencies would need pinning (not preinstalled).\n- **Risks to feasibility of a real run** (not blocking, since design-only): tokenizer unavailability (mitigated by a pretokenized/deterministic local corpus [ASSUMPTION]); estimator instability at k\u2192small matrices (mitigated by cross-checks S4 and the noise floor).\n\n---\n\n## Consolidated assumptions (all consequential choices, explicitly not confirmed facts)\n1. **BAdam variant** = block-coordinate AdamW (Luo et al. 2024), D=6 layer-blocks, cyclic schedule, 1-cycle coordinator warm-up, default M=1 inner step.\n2. **Model/task** = 6-layer d_model=192 GPT-2-style decoder, ~6.5M params, next-token LM on \u224810M tokens (TinyStories or a local/deterministic corpus due to no network / no shipped datasets).\n3. **Modules** = Q/K/V/O + up/gate/down projection matrices (42 matrices); embeddings, un-tied LM head excluded (appendix only); all 1-D params excluded.\n4. **Aligned comparison = per-cycle/per-pass**, primary; per-step is a caveated sensitivity condition (per-